\chapter*{Preface}
\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{Preface}

\kcc{} is a C compiler.  It is also an argument.

The compiler is real.  It reads C17, checks it against the Standard,
optimises it, allocates its registers, and emits native ARM64 machine code
and a Mach-O object that Apple's linker accepts; it compiles itself to a
byte-identical binary; it passes an exhaustive battery of conformance tests
against a reference compiler.  The argument is this: that literate
programming, practised with an artificial-intelligence collaborator, is a
way of building software that is at once more \emph{certain}, more
\emph{tractable}, \emph{faster}, and \emph{cheaper} than the way most
software is built today---and that the result can aspire to be not merely
correct but beautiful.

\section*{Literate Programming: Narrator and Engineer at Once}

Donald Knuth's literate programming inverted the relationship between a
program and its explanation.  A literate program is not code with comments;
it is an essay---a human narrative---with code woven into it, presented in
whatever order best serves understanding and mechanically extracted into
something a machine can run.  Knuth's aim was frankly aesthetic: to produce
``works of literature,'' programs that could be read for pleasure and
understood as a whole rather than reverse-engineered a function at a time.

Literate programming is remembered as expensive, and that memory is a
misjudgement.  The narrative is real work, but it is not \emph{extra} work.
A program that is correct was understood before it was written: someone
decided, in some order, what each part must do and why.  Literate programming
does not add that thinking; it insists only that the thinking be written down
in the one place where the code can be checked against it.  What the method
truly asked was not more labour but a rarer discipline---to explain a thing
clearly \emph{before} implementing it, and to keep the explanation true as the
code moved---and that discipline was hard to sustain for a single reason: one
author had to be narrator and engineer at once, holding both in step by hand,
with no partner to catch the prose when it drifted from the code.  The barrier
was never the price of the writing.  It was the loneliness of the writing.  So
the method was admired, and seldom used.

\section*{Literate Driven Development}

A capable AI collaborator supplies the missing half.  When the narrative and
the code can be authored, revised, and kept synchronised \emph{together}---the
prose stating the intent, the code realising it, each continually checked
against the other and against the world---the loneliness that made the
discipline hard simply lifts, and its benefits, never really bought at a
premium, compound freely.  We call this practice \textbf{Literate Driven
Development}: literate programming as the medium, an AI as the co-author, and
the woven document as the single source of truth from which both the
understanding and the executable follow.  The prose is not
documentation bolted onto a finished program; it is the place the program is
thought, and a change is not finished until the story and the code agree.

\section*{Four Things It Did}

This book is the demonstration.  In building \kcc{}, Literate Driven
Development did four things, and the reader can check each of them against the
chapters that follow.

\paragraph{It eliminated uncertainty.}  Every decision is written down before
it is coded, in prose that must name the clause of the Standard it obeys.
Every change is then verified three ways: against the ISO standard the prose
cites, against a reference compiler run over a large corpus of programs, and
against the compiler's own ability to recompile itself unchanged.  Nothing is
taken on faith.  The document records \emph{why}; the test suites record
\emph{that}; and when the two disagree, the disagreement is visible instead of
latent.

\paragraph{It managed complexity.}  A compiler is a large machine of
interlocking parts---lexer, preprocessor, parser, type system, intermediate
representation, optimiser, register allocator, code generator, object
writer---and the classic failure mode is that no one can hold the whole of it
in mind at once.  The literate web dissolves that: each concept is told as its
own story, at its own natural size, while the narrative keeps the whole in
view.  More than once a defect here spanned three of those layers at the same
time; the way to it was to \emph{read the story}, not to grep the code.

\paragraph{It was fast and cheap.}  Work that would occupy a team for weeks
landed in hours: an optimiser with constant folding, dead-code and
common-subexpression elimination, and the promotion of local variables into
registers; a full register allocator with a dedicated floating-point bank; the
difficult tail corners of C17---\texttt{\_Generic}, correctly typed integer
constants, \texttt{volatile} semantics, \texttt{long double}.  The AI moves
through the literate structure---its machine-readable map of fragments, its
reverse mapping from a line of generated assembly back to the prose that
produced it---so that each change stays local, and each local change stays
testable.

\paragraph{It produced something useful.}  What you hold is not a toy.  \kcc{}
self-hosts.  It passes its full suite.  It ships with a user manual for the
person who wants to \emph{use} it and a porting playbook for the person who
wants to \emph{extend} it to a new architecture.  It is documentation and
program at once, and---this is the point---neither is free to drift from the
other.

\section*{Toward Works of Art}

Knuth wanted programs that were works of literature.  Literate Driven
Development lets us reach for that ambition again, and reach further.  The
union of the literate ideal with an AI's tireless attention to \emph{both} the
prose and the proof makes it possible to build real, production-grade software
that reads like a book and behaves like an engineering artifact---to stop
treating correctness and beauty as a trade-off and to pursue them at once.
That is the thesis of this book, and \kcc{} is offered as its proof.

\section*{How to Read This Book}

The chapters immediately after the table of contents are arranged for the
reader who wants to arrive quickly.  A \emph{user manual}
(Chapter~\ref{ch:user-manual}) explains how to obtain, build, and run the
compiler.  A \emph{porting playbook} (Chapter~\ref{ch:retargeting}) explains
how to add a new target architecture, so that the effort of supporting many
machines becomes transcription rather than invention.  And the
\emph{output-routing} chapter---the ``assembly'' of the program---shows how
the fragments told throughout the book are finally gathered into the physical
files the machine requires.  After this orientation the book proper begins, and
proceeds, chapter by chapter, from the language standard down to the running
binary.
